AB  - Baseline correction of ground motions has long been seen as an open problem due to insufficient understanding as well as the absence of trustworthy recorded ground rotations. Current baseline correction methods deal with the problem by focusing on extracting drift trends from integrated velocity time-histories. Due to the relatively direct sensitivity of ground motion recovery to the selection of the time points used in obtaining the drift trends, baseline correction of ground motions is considered unrobust. With the widely recognized assumption that ground tilt is the dominant factor of baseline drift, we proposed the tilt correction pattern. Based on our non-tilt 2-dimensional shake table tests, we found the existence of cumulative velocity errors and we proposed the cumulative correction pattern to address this problem. Our recent studies reveal that a sufficient recording length is essential for proper baseline corrections. Our studies on the 2018 Mw 6.4 Hualien earthquake and the most recent 2023 Mw 7.8 and Mw 7.6 Turkey earthquake doublet suggest that the majority of recorded accelerograms can be properly recovered where ground motion recoveries are consistent with geodetic r…
